                <pb facs="0457" n="351" sameAs="frus:frus1906p1/pg351"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Chargé Coolidge to the Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram—Paraphrase.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1906-11-07"><hi rendition="#italic">November 7,
                        1906</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>(Mr. Coolidge reports that important edicts are being issued changing the
                    structure of the government and preparing the way for <pb facs="0458" n="352"
                        xml:id="pg352"/> constitutional government, and mentions, among the changes
                    thus far made, the following: The consolidation of minor departments with
                    boards, each of which is to have only one responsible head; the creation of a
                    new board of communications controlling the postal service, telegraph service,
                    railways, and steamships, and the prospective appointment of two councils, one
                    to consider the national budget, the other to receive expressions of public
                    opinion and deliberate thereon.)</p>
            </div>
        </body>
